Every rooted directory forms one structural module. Three optional authored root files give that module its roles. `@mod.moth` publishes the normal API facade, `@package.moth` publishes the scoped support facade or the external project package surface, and `@entry.moth` owns executable entry work.

### The three root files

[table:
    [th:
        [: Root file]
        [: Structural role]
        [: Publishes API]
        [: Implicit `start`]
        [: Top-level runtime and fragments]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `@mod.moth`]
        [: Normal public facade]
        [: Yes]
        [: No]
        [: Rejected]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `@package.moth`]
        [: Scoped support or external project package facade]
        [: Yes]
        [: No]
        [: Rejected]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `@entry.moth`]
        [: Executable entry root]
        [: No]
        [: Yes]
        [: Permitted]
    ]
]

The filenames are exact and case-sensitive. A directory may contain any present subset, including all three files. `@Entry.moth` is not an entry root. An entry-only directory is a valid module even though it publishes no importable API.

Ordinary `.moth` files hold shared implementation. They never own a facade, a start function or an entry.

### One structural module per directory

A rooted directory has one `ModuleId`, one compilation and one atomic publish-or-diagnose outcome. Its `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` and `@entry.moth`, when present, are three root-local views of that one module. They are not three modules.

A directory containing only `config.moth` is not rooted. `config.moth` remains the optional build-only project config and stays outside the module graph.

A root marker may be a symlink, but every root must resolve to a distinct physical file owned by its module. The visible symlink name decides the role. Two markers cannot alias one file, and a root cannot resolve into another structural module.

### The root vocabulary is closed

Only `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` and `@entry.moth` are authored root files. Any other `.moth` filename whose stem starts with `@`, `+` or `#` is a Stage 0 structure error:

[codeblock, $code "text":
    @page.moth          rejected
    @home.moth          rejected
    @api.moth           rejected
    @Entry.moth         rejected
    +package.moth       rejected
    #package.moth       rejected
    @config.moth        rejected
]

The diagnostic is `MOTH-CONFIG-0001` (`InvalidModuleRootFileName`). It names the invalid filename and lists the three accepted root files. It does not guess whether the author meant an entry, a normal facade or a support facade.

The rejected spellings are never reclassified as ordinary helper files. `config.moth` without a leading `@` keeps its config meaning. Non-`.moth` files with an `@` prefix, such as `@theme.css`, remain ordinary resources.

A `+` prefix is not a root marker in a non-`.moth` filename. An ordinary supported source such as `+notes.mtf` resolves through the same dependency path and visibility rules as any other helper source.

### Root-local declarations stay in their root

Each root file is its own declaration and visibility scope. A top-level declaration in one root is visible only in that file:

- another root cannot resolve it
- ordinary helper files cannot resolve it
- re-exporting it through another root is rejected
- naming the root file in a dependency path is rejected

The rejection keeps the root-local declaration site as a related location. Ordinary helpers still supply the shared implementation that every root may bind.

Shared helper declarations are prepared once and carry one shared origin. A root imports them through ordinary dependency clauses. Helpers obey ordinary same-module declaration ordering and cannot depend on any root-local declaration.

Same-spelling declarations in disjoint roots stay independent. A name that conflicts with a shared helper already in scope is an ordinary visible-name collision.

[codeblock, $code "text":
    site/
    ├── @mod.moth          public facade
    ├── @package.moth      support facade
    ├── @entry.moth        executable entry
    └── shared.moth        helper bound by all three roots
]

In this layout `shared.moth` can expose `site_label` to every root, but no root can read either sibling root's private declarations.

### What each root may contain

`@mod.moth` and `@package.moth` carry declarations and function bodies. They may use an `export:` block to publish their API. They reject top-level runtime statements, direct const fragments, runtime fragments and builder-slot contributions. A rejected top-level runtime body reports `MOTH-RULE-0023`. Functions declared inside a facade remain ordinary callable functions when reachable through the API.

`@entry.moth` carries private declarations, top-level runtime statements, direct compile-time and runtime fragments and registered direct builder slots. It owns the module's implicit `start`. It rejects `export:` and any future export modifier.

An API facade has no implicit start, route metadata or entry lifecycle. An entry root publishes no importable interface. Only the two facades publish APIs.

### Root identity and dependency names

The authored root filename never supplies a dependency name. Names come from the containing directory or the registered package identity.

[codeblock, $code "text":
    @entry.moth
    pages/
    ├── @mod.moth
    └── article.moth
]

Valid dependency from a sibling module:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @pages page_heading
]

Invalid dependency spellings:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @@pages
    @@mod
]

Do not concatenate the filesystem `@` with the dependency `@`. The exact root filename is the role, not a dependency name. Direct dependency clauses on a root file are rejected. Depend on the module directory instead.

### Normal files belong to the surrounding module

Ordinary files in the same directory belong to that module. They contribute shared declarations that any root may bind. They do not execute top-level runtime work independently, and they never become entry roots by themselves.

### Entry eligibility follows presence

A module is entry-eligible when `@entry.moth` exists. Presence alone decides eligibility. An empty file, a file with only static content and a file with only builder metadata all count. No activity heuristic selects an HTML route.

Compiling or importing a module never activates its entry. Only the selected build command activates a compiled entry, and a project-local `@entry.moth` is the only project entry source. Separately compiled source packages and dependency entries never activate.

### Visibility status

`export:` is the implemented public-API block. It is legal only in `@mod.moth` and `@package.moth`; an entry or ordinary helper that uses it is rejected with `MOTH-RULE-0077`. The accepted future `$export` migration will replace this spelling, restricted to the same two facades. It will never apply to `@entry.moth`, helpers, templates or config. Do not use `$export` as current syntax.

### Concepts related to module roots

- For the export block and its current status see @#public-api (Public API).
- For entry runtime and fragments see @#entry-runtime-and-fragments (Entry runtime and fragments).
- For HTML routes and command policy see @#html-routing-and-artifacts (HTML routing and artefacts).
